**Definition 2.1 (Cramer coordinates).** For $t=(t_{ijk})\in\mathbb C^8$, define the slices $T_j=(t_{ijk})_{i,k=0,1}$ and
$$
\tag{4}
 K(t)=\begin{pmatrix}
 t_{000}&t_{001}&-t_{000}&-t_{100}\\
 t_{010}&t_{011}&-t_{001}&-t_{101}\\
 t_{100}&t_{101}&-t_{010}&-t_{110}\\
 t_{110}&t_{111}&-t_{011}&-t_{111}
 \end{pmatrix}.
$$
Let $H$ be its upper-left three-by-three submatrix, $d=\det H$, and
let $c_j$ be the determinant obtained by replacing column $j$ of $H$
by $-K_{\{0,1,2\},3}$, for $j=0,1,2$. Set
$$
\begin{aligned}
 \widetilde\ell&=(c_2,d),& \widetilde z&=(c_0,c_1)^T,&
 W_{ik}&=\sum_{j=0}^1\widetilde\ell_jt_{jik},\\
 N_j&=T_j\operatorname{adj} W,&J&=N_0+N_1,&\widetilde r&=W\widetilde z.
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}
$$
All these quantities are polynomial in $t$. Our domain is specified by
$$
\tag{6}
 g=d\det W\det J\det T_0\,
 (t_{000}t_{011}-t_{001}t_{010})\ne0.
$$
On this domain put
$$
\tag{7}
 \ell=\widetilde\ell/d,\quad z=\widetilde z/d,\quad B=W/d,
 \quad M_j=T_jB^{-1},\quad r=Bz,\quad C=M_0+M_1.
$$
Then $B$ and $C$ are invertible, since
$M_j=dN_j/\det W$ and $C=dJ/\det W$. The inverse in Theorem 1 is
$$
\tag{8}
 F_{N,a}(t)_w=\ell C^{-a}M_{w_1}\cdots M_{w_N}C^{-b}r,
 \qquad b=N-3-a.
$$

**Proof of Theorem 1.** 
*The quartic relation.*
First take $t_{ijk}=lA_iA_jA_kr$. Define
$P_{i,*}=lA_i$ and $Q_{*,k}=A_kr$, so $T_j=PA_jQ$.
On the open set where $P,Q$ are invertible, put
$e=lP^{-1}$ and $v=Q^{-1}r$. For each $i,j$ one has
$$
\tag{9}
 \sum_k t_{ijk}v_k=lA_iA_jr=\sum_k e_kt_{kij}.
$$
Consequently $K(t)(v_0,v_1,e_0,e_1)^T=0$. This vector is nonzero since
$Q$ invertible implies $r\ne0$. Thus $\det K(t)=0$ on that parameter open
set. It is nonempty: $A_0=I$, $A_1=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$,
$l=(1,0)$, $r=(1,0)^T$ give $P=Q=I$. The parameter space is affine
and irreducible, so the polynomial $\det K(t)$ vanishes for all parameters,
and therefore on $X_3$.

*Three-site reconstruction.*
Cramer's rule applied to the first three rows of $K$ gives
$$
\tag{10}
 K(t)(c_0,c_1,c_2,d)^T=(0,0,0,\det K(t))^T.
$$
The last component follows by expanding the determinant in its last row.
For $t\in U$, division by $d$ gives
$$
\tag{11}
 \sum_k t_{ijk}z_k=\sum_k\ell_kt_{kij}=B_{ij}.
$$
The definition of $B$ also gives $\ell T_i=B_{i,*}$. Hence
$$
\tag{12}
 \ell M_i=e_i^T,\qquad M_kr=T_kz=B_{*,k},\qquad
 \ell M_iM_jM_kr=(M_jB)_{ik}=t_{ijk}.
$$
Here $e_i$ is the $i$th coordinate column. Thus the reconstructed matrices
and boundaries give exactly $t$ as a three-site tensor.

*A right inverse for every length and position.*
Equation (8) is itself of the form (1); it
therefore belongs to the parametrized $N$-site model. Summing over the
omitted sites multiplies its boundaries by $C^a$ and $C^b$.
These powers cancel their inverses, and (12) yields
$\mu_aF_{N,a}(t)=t$ for every $t\in U$.

*Uniqueness on parametrized tensors.*
Let $t=\mu_a\psi\in U$ with $\psi$ as in (1).
Use $l=\alpha S^a$, $r_0=S^b\beta$, and form $P,Q$ as above with
$r_0$ in place of $r$. Since $\det T_0\ne0$ and $T_0=PA_0Q$,
both $P,Q$ are invertible. Define $e=lP^{-1}$ and $v=Q^{-1}r_0$.
The first three rows of $K$ are independent, because $d\ne0$.
Their kernel, and hence the kernel of $K$, is the line spanned by
$(c_0,c_1,c_2,d)^T$. The nonzero vector $(v,e)$ lies on this line.
Thus $s=e_1\ne0$, $\ell=s^{-1}e$, and $z=s^{-1}v$.
It follows directly that
$$
\tag{13}
 B=s^{-1}PQ,\quad M_j=sPA_jP^{-1},\quad
 r=s^{-2}Pr_0,\quad C=sPSP^{-1}.
$$
Invertibility of $C$ implies invertibility of $S$. Substituting
(13) in (8) cancels the gauge matrices and
gives the scalar factor
$s^{-1-a+N-b-2}=1$, since $N=a+b+3$. The remaining expression is
$\alpha A_{w_1}\cdots A_{w_N}\beta$. Therefore
$F_{N,a}(\mu_a\psi)=\psi$ for every such parametrized tensor.

*Closure and nonemptiness.*
Each $X_N$ is the closure of a polynomial image of an affine space,
so is irreducible. Equivalently, its vanishing ideal is the kernel of
the substitution homomorphism into the polynomial ring in the entries
of $A_0,A_1,\alpha,\beta$. This ring is an integral domain, and a
polynomial vanishing at all complex parameter values is zero. The
kernel, and hence the vanishing ideal, is prime.

The three-site tensor furnished by
$$
\tag{14}
 A_0=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\3&4\end{pmatrix},\quad
 A_1=\begin{pmatrix}5&6\\7&8\end{pmatrix},\quad
 l=(1,0),\quad r_0=(1,2)^T
$$
has lexicographically ordered coordinates
$(145,349,337,829,489,1173,1193,2933)$. Its five factors in
(6) are respectively
$$
 387072,\quad172598092627968,\quad-397666005414838272,
 \quad-576,\quad2592.
$$
Thus $U$ is nonempty. Its inverse image under $\mu_a$ is nonempty by
the right-inverse construction. The parametrized tensors are dense in
$X_N$, and their intersection with this principal open subset is dense
in that subset. Both $F_{N,a}\mu_a$ and the identity are regular there,
so their coordinatewise equality extends from these parametrized tensors
to the whole subset. The two displayed maps are therefore inverse
isomorphisms of nonempty open subsets of the irreducible varieties.
 $\square$

For explicit polynomial numerators and denominator, put $v=\det W$,
$c=\det J$. Formula (8) becomes
$$
\tag{15}
 F_{N,a}(t)_w=
 \frac{\widetilde\ell(\operatorname{adj} J)^aN_{w_1}\cdots N_{w_N}
 (\operatorname{adj} J)^b\widetilde r}{v^3c^{N-3}}.
$$
Indeed $\ell=d^{-1}\widetilde\ell$,
$r=d^{-2}\widetilde r$, $M_j=(d/v)N_j$, and
$C^{-1}=(v/(dc))\operatorname{adj} J$; the powers of $d$ cancel because $a+b=N-3$.
The denominator is nonzero throughout $U$. This also makes the
regularity used in the proof explicit.

